WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.760
It felt like a Wix ad was on every YouTube video.&nbsp;

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:10.160
Wix was one of the biggest website builders,&nbsp;
so big they’ve run many superbowl ads.&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:15.200
But now it’s dying.
Their value is down 90%.&nbsp;

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:21.280
From $19 billion, to about $2 billion.
And now, they’re being investigated for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:27.440
potentially misleading investors.
What happened to Wix?

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:38.240
Wix has a pretty typical founder’s story.
Developers Avishai Abrahami, his brother Nadav,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:43.600
and Giora Kaplan, were actually working on&nbsp;
a different business, when they noticed how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:49.760
annoying and difficult it was to build a website.
But where there’s problems or frustration, there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:56.240
often opportunity, if you provide a solution.
This sounds good, but it needs a tiny bit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:57.360
context.
Tension&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.280
Wix wasn’t first to website&nbsp;
building. Not by a mile.&nbsp;

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:06.400
There was already Squarespace, WordPress,&nbsp;
while GeoCities and Adobe Dreamweaver had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.920
been around since the 90s. Weebly also&nbsp;
launched about the same time as Wix.&nbsp;

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.800
Though, Wix did have something going&nbsp;
for it. Specifically, three things.&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:22.400
For one, it was very customizable, which&nbsp;
goes back to the founder’s frustration.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:28.480
Squarespace had better templates, but Wix gave you&nbsp;
more freedom, since it was drag and drop. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:34.240
had an idea in your head, you could create it.
This made Wix pretty unique. Even if some other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:40.160
platforms didn’t need coding, it was still&nbsp;
the easiest to jump into and start building.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.720
But, ironically, this freedom&nbsp;
came with a very big downside.&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:04.000
It was built using Adobe Flash.
Not HTML like Dreamweaver or PHP like WordPress.&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.560
This allowed for more freedom,&nbsp;
but boy did Flash have downsides.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.760
Flash had a lot of security vulnerabilities.
It had notably bad SEO.&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.880
On mobile, it drained battery, had&nbsp;
no touch support, and was unreliable.&nbsp;

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:40.160
It was so bad, that Steve Jobs refused to&nbsp;
let Flash websites run on the iPhone. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:45.200
even published an open letter criticizing it.
“Flash has been such a problem because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:51.360
way that it hooks into systems, it’s been a virus&nbsp;
nightmare on Windows, even on the Mac. And when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:58.080
we got it running on iOS, the performance was just&nbsp;
abysmal and embarrassing and it could never get to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:04.960
something which would be consumer value add.”
From 2012, Wix adopted HTML5, but still had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:08.560
to support Flash for years,&nbsp;
because of preexisting users.&nbsp;

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.400
Though their “drag and drop”&nbsp;
model, was definitely a hit.&nbsp;

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:17.600
Squarespace followed suit in 2014,&nbsp;
adding their own drag and drop system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.160
But it sure wasn’t with Flash.
Wix was very much catered to your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:27.520
small business owners, or individuals who just&nbsp;
wanted a website quickly. No coding background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:32.320
and little interest in it. The drag and&nbsp;
drop, naturally seems quite appealing.&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:38.720
Wix was freemium, so they preferred having lots&nbsp;
and lots of free users, with limited features,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:43.440
who might upgrade to all the premium&nbsp;
features: Ecommerce, custom domains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.560
a business email, booking, more storage.
Wix was also pretty early to this trend.&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:51.840
And, they were very concerned&nbsp;
with “wowing” customers.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.480
Wix catered to those who wanted something quick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.045
easy, and, most importantly: cheap.
“my SO is a freelance web design/build person The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.045 --> 00:03:57.144
types of clients she loses to Wix or Squarespace&nbsp;
are the worst clients. They want her to do a site&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.144 --> 00:03:57.243
for $200, or for "the exposure... I'll let you put&nbsp;
your name on it!" They are the least profitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.243 --> 00:03:57.340
and most fussy. Then she's got clients like the&nbsp;
local high-end interior decorator who is totally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:57.440
fine paying $5k for a really nice, customized site&nbsp;
and then paying hourly for upkeep and updates.”

