00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:11.840
For now, Wix was growing fast.
In early 2010, they had 3.5 million users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:16.400
by around the same time in 2011,&nbsp;
that had jumped to 8.5 million.&nbsp;

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:21.920
And with that came funding.
$10 million in 2010, $40 million in 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:29.920
an IPO in 2013, raising another $127 million.
This website builder, now had a lot of cash.&nbsp;

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:42.000
And that meant, a lot of money for ads.
Though, some were much weirder than others.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:14.480
This was Wix’s ad for the 2015 Super Bowl.
But they weren’t the only website brand there.

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:25.680
Each of these ads, 30-second&nbsp;
spots, cost $4.5 million.&nbsp;

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:30.000
These website builders were becoming more&nbsp;
than just SaaS tools. They were becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.633
huge brands, with huge marketing budgets.
[Eric Mason, Wix, Director of US Marketing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.633 --> 00:07:38.080
2015]: "We want people to understand who we&nbsp;
are as a household name and as a major brand"&nbsp;

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.299
Squarespace had the same idea:
[Anthony Casalena, Founder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.299 --> 00:07:43.600
CEO of Squarespace]: the same year:&nbsp;
"We want people to have heard of us."&nbsp;

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:47.680
And weirdly, a bit of an arms&nbsp;
race began between these two.

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:53.200
Wix did the same again in 2016,&nbsp;
and even partnered with DreamWorks.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:13.440
I can’t imagine how much this would’ve cost.
Then they did another in 2017, this one over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:17.680
a minute, featuring Jason Statham.
It was working though.&nbsp;

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:23.920
This company was everywhere. It felt like&nbsp;
it was advertised on every YouTube video.&nbsp;

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:28.720
But not all of their marketing campaigns&nbsp;
were hits. Some, were huge misses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:34.320
and got them into a lot of drama.
But first we need some context.&nbsp;

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:41.920
Around this time, Wix ran into a bit of a problem.
In October 2016, Matt Mullenweg, co-founder of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:46.622
the open source software WordPress,&nbsp;
publishes an open letter to Wix.&nbsp;

00:08:46.622 --> 00:08:51.840
[Matt Mullenweg]: “Wix copied WordPress without&nbsp;
attribution, credit, or following the license. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:57.840
custom icons, the class names, even the bugs. You&nbsp;
can see the forked repositories on GitHub complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:03.040
with original commits from Alex and Maxime,&nbsp;
two developers on Automattic’s mobile team.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:08.480
This blatant rip-off and code theft is beyond&nbsp;
anything I’ve seen before from a competitor.”&nbsp;

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:13.360
Wix’s CEO Abrahami, responds.
“Wow, dude I did not even know we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:20.400
were fighting.” He argues the code isn’t stolen.
“ Here is a list of 224 projects on our public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:28.000
GitHub page, and as you can see they are all&nbsp;
dated before your post. Yes, we did use the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:33.680
WordPress open source library for a minor part&nbsp;
of the application (that is the concept of open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:40.080
source right?), and everything we improved there&nbsp;
or modified, we submitted back as open source”&nbsp;

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:43.520
But Mullenweg wasn’t satisfied.
[Matt Mullenweg]:“Releasing other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:47.520
open source projects doesn’t mean that&nbsp;
you can violate the license of the editor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:53.600
code you distributed in your mobile apps.”
Ultimately, these two resolved this in private,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:58.880
in person. So why does this matter?
Because, what makes this especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:03.760
weird… is that this wouldn’t be the&nbsp;
last feud Wix would have with WordPress.&nbsp;

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:09.200
In late 2020, Wix sent out a bunch of Bose&nbsp;
headphones to notable WordPress users.

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:14.400
But inside, was a QR code, signed from&nbsp;
WordPress, which linked to a video.&nbsp;

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.440
Unfortunately, I can’t find the video,&nbsp;

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:22.400
But it essentially had a “top secret”&nbsp;
video featuring a caricature of WordPress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:27.440
and potentially Mullenweg, acting&nbsp;
like a toxic, over-possessive ex.

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:32.480
Yeah, this didn’t go down well.
Not only was it in bad taste,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:38.400
but it was also confusing. Some people&nbsp;
thought it was actually from WordPress.&nbsp;

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:43.120
Developers started to make fun of Wix.
“Wix is flushing money down the drain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:49.120
with that Bose campaign. Such a waste of&nbsp;
money. They'll make a nice regift though.”&nbsp;

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:53.520
And some even made fun of their&nbsp;
broken web design on the video page.&nbsp;

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:58.800
Then Mullenweg got onboard, and&nbsp;
oh boy did it get so much worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:02.640
when he pointed out Wix’s other&nbsp;
problems, like Wix’s lock-in.

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:07.680
“They’re like a roach motel where&nbsp;
you can check in but never check out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:13.360
Once you buy into their proprietary stack you’re&nbsp;
locked in, which even their support documentation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:20.160
admits. So if we’re comparing website builders to&nbsp;
abusive relationships, Wix is one that locks you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:24.800
in the basement and doesn’t let you leave.&nbsp;
I’m surprised consumer protection agencies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:27.920
haven’t gone after them.”
He’s right though.&nbsp;

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:33.280
Wix very much wanted you to use only Wix,&nbsp;
even if they didn’t have what you needed.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:00.880
But, it was about to get a whole lot worse.

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:07.920
If you’ve watched this far, consider subscribing!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:11.600
You probably enjoy our stuff, and&nbsp;
it helps the channel too! Thanks.

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:17.680
Around this time, something else&nbsp;
was happening. The pandemic.&nbsp;

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:25.600
From January 2020 to August 2021, Wix&nbsp;
shares shot up 144%, peaking on February 19,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:32.640
2021 at a $20B valuation. But you&nbsp;
can probably guess what’s coming.&nbsp;

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:36.160
The surge of pandemic growth,&nbsp;
eventually evaporated.&nbsp;

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:41.760
Q2 2021 was a $37.6M profit, but Q2 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:47.600
was a $111.2M net loss.
But it gets worse.

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:53.680
Remember Wix’s freemium model?
They had a lot of users. 222 million using their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.680 --> 00:13:01.040
tools. But, only 6 million were premium customers.
A conversion rate of 2.7%.&nbsp;

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:08.720
Wix’s stock was collapsing, in late 2022 it&nbsp;
was down 79% from peak, and down 54% that year.&nbsp;

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:15.600
So, they laid out a plan.
25% free cash flow margin by 2025.&nbsp;

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:19.920
To do so, they announced $150&nbsp;
million a year in cost cutting.&nbsp;

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:23.920
It was around this time that&nbsp;
activist investors had moved in.&nbsp;

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:31.680
But, surprisingly, this turnaround did work.
Wix was soon profitable. By 2024, the stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:37.280
had recovered, and a lot at that,&nbsp;
though not quite to pandemic peaks.&nbsp;

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:44.480
Their free cash flow margin in 2024 was 28%.&nbsp;
“Nearly double in 2024 compared to previous year”&nbsp;

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:48.080
Earlier than expected, and&nbsp;
above the original goal.&nbsp;

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:54.640
Q4 revenue was $460 million, up 14% as well.
This was great.&nbsp;

00:13:54.640 --> 00:14:03.600
But soon after, the stock began to crash. Why?
Well Wix had a new problem. Perhaps the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:06.960
they had ever faced.
AI.&nbsp;

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:11.840
AI was, and still is, rapidly&nbsp;
changing the website industry.&nbsp;

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:16.640
Why pay for Wix to build a website, when&nbsp;
you can do it with the many AI tools? And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:21.840
do it faster with more customization.
The already saturated website maker market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:25.840
got a whole lot more competitive.
Wix needed to respond.&nbsp;

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:30.000
They bought Base44, a 6 month&nbsp;
old, vibe coding platform.&nbsp;

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.400
For $80 million.
Wix also promised some “transformative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:40.880
products”, for spring and early fall 2025.
Wixel, an AI design platform to compete with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:44.960
Canva and Adobe Express.
But more importantly:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:50.320
Wix Harmony, an AI website builder.
But, Harmony was delayed. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:55.600
when it finally did release, customers&nbsp;
found it was slow, and was missing features.

00:14:55.600 --> 00:15:00.880
Product updates were also delayed.
The Wix Harmony ad in the Super Bowl wasn’t really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:08.560
landing well. But not because it dealt with AI.
“This ad feels like it arrived a year late-maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:14.320
two. The idea that AI can help you build a website&nbsp;
faster, easier, and with fewer technical skills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:21.760
was compelling in 2024, still serviceable in&nbsp;
2025, but in 2026, it feels table stakes.”&nbsp;

00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:27.520
Especially bad, since Squarespace also had a&nbsp;
Super Bowl ad, which was basically a cinematic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:31.440
horror short film, starring&nbsp;
Emma Stone. A very creative ad.&nbsp;

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:35.360
Wix, felt like it was falling&nbsp;
behind, on the race it had bet on.&nbsp;

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:40.960
Then, came the 2025 financial&nbsp;
results, which were… A bit concerning.

00:15:40.960 --> 00:15:46.720
Their earnings and revenue were also below&nbsp;
expectations, and operating margins had declined.&nbsp;

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:51.920
Operating expenses had jumped 46%.
They also said “Expect free cash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:57.520
flow margin in the low-to-mid-20%&nbsp;
range in 2026”. A decent step down.

00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:03.360
Wix’s stock dropped another 27%, and&nbsp;
shortly after, came the investigations.

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:08.640
BFA is investigating whether Wix made false and&nbsp;
misleading statements to investors regarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:12.880
demand from professional designers, AI&nbsp;
competition, and its ability to deliver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:17.280
new products and innovation to sustain growth.
“There had been delays in delivering product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:21.760
updates and innovation to professional&nbsp;
developer customers, and as a result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.760 --> 00:16:26.480
the Company had fallen behind "the workflow&nbsp;
and the needs of" professional developers.”

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:33.520
Wix then announced major layoffs. 20% of&nbsp;
their total workforce, over 1,000 people.&nbsp;

00:16:33.520 --> 00:16:41.200
Abrahami said this was due to “ fast evolution of&nbsp;
AI capabilities”, but, I’m not so sure about that.&nbsp;

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:46.080
I think the company is struggling, trying to&nbsp;
cut back costs, and is hiding it under “AI”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:50.480
to make it seem as if they’re “putting more&nbsp;
money towards AI”, like other companies are.

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:56.560
Either way, the future looks rough for Wix.
Their market cap is down about 90%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.560 --> 00:17:02.720
their business model is crumbling, and to&nbsp;
top it off, they also have a ton of debt.

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:08.160
Wix isn’t the only one feeling this pressure.
Another SaaS company that was all over YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:12.800
and is now struggling, is Monday.com.
And like Wix, they may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:22.480
hidden the problems from investors…
